## 917: Triangle Euler diagram (one task)

Let A be equilateral triangles, B isosceles triangles, and C all
nondegenerate Euclidean triangles.

Inclusive definition, "at least two equal sides": draw the A region wholly
inside B and B wholly inside C, leaving both surrounding differences nonempty.
Thus A is a proper subset of B, B is a proper subset of C, and
A intersect B = A. Every equilateral triangle has at least two equal sides.
Side lengths (3,3,3) witness A; (5,5,6) witness B minus A; (3,4,5) witness
C minus B. Each example satisfies the strict triangle inequalities.

Strict definition, "exactly two equal sides": draw disjoint A and B regions
inside C, leaving the scalene region outside both. Then A intersect B is
empty, and A union B is a proper subset of C. The same three witnesses occupy
A, B, and the exterior of both, respectively.

No shading is requested. The raw source does not settle which definition
is intended, so both diagrams are retained as conditional solutions.

## 918: Rectangles, Rhombi, and Shading (one task)

Draw overlapping regions C (rectangles) and D (rhombi) in a quadrilateral
universe. Neither contains the other. Hatch exactly the entire overlap,
C intersect D, representing all squares. Do not hatch either exclusive
region or the exterior.

A square has both four right angles and four equal sides. Conversely, a
rectangle that is also a rhombus has both properties and is a square, so the
intersection is exactly the square set. A 3-by-2 rectangle witnesses C minus D.
A rhombus spanned by (5,0) and (3,4) witnesses D minus C: both adjacent
lengths are 5, but their dot product is 15, not zero. A side-2 square witnesses
the intersection. Diagram construction and its requested shading together
count as this one task.

## 919: Five Quadrilateral Sets (one task)

Draw A (convex quadrilaterals) as the outer region, with B (parallelograms)
wholly inside. Place overlapping C (rectangles) and D (rhombi) wholly inside B.
Label their entire overlap F (squares); F is not an independent extra region.
No shading is requested.

The relations are B proper-subset A, C proper-subset B, D proper-subset B,
and F = C intersect D, with F a proper subset of both C and D.
All five regions below must remain nonempty:

| Region | Vertices in cyclic order |
| --- | --- |
| F | (0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2) |
| C minus D | (0,0),(3,0),(3,2),(0,2) |
| D minus C | (0,0),(5,0),(8,4),(3,4) |
| B minus (C union D) | (0,0),(4,0),(5,2),(1,2) |
| A minus B | (0,0),(4,0),(3,2),(0,2) |

Rectangles and rhombi are parallelograms; parallelograms are convex.
The right-angle and equal-side conditions together define squares.
These witnesses also refute C subset D, D subset C, B = C union D, and A = B.

## 920: Relative Complements (four tasks)

For each task, remove the named subset only from its stated ambient set.

| Task | Complement | Included witness | Excluded witness |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | Rectangles that are not squares | 3-by-2 rectangle | side-2 square |
| b | All non-equilateral triangles | (5,5,6) and (3,4,5) | (3,3,3) |
| c | Rhombi that are not squares | rhombus spanned by (5,0),(3,4) | side-2 square |
| d | {3k+1:k in Z} union {3k+2:k in Z} | 1,2,-1,-2 | 0,3,-3 |

For a, a rectangle is non-square exactly when its adjacent side lengths differ.
For b, a non-equilateral triangle has exactly two equal side lengths or all
distinct side lengths. In particular, the complement is not just scalene
triangles, regardless of the naming convention in 917.
For c, the remaining rhombi have non-right angles. A non-right rhombus is not
in the ambient set of a; a non-square rectangle is not in the ambient set of c.
For d, the division algorithm n = 3q+r with r in {0,1,2} removes exactly r=0.
Negative integers are included: -1=3(-1)+2 and -2=3(-1)+1. Zero is removed.

## 921: Closure of {-1,0,1} (three tasks)

Closure quantifies over every ordered pair in the set.

| Task | Operation | Verdict | Complete reason |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | Addition | False | 1+1=2 is outside the set. |
| b | Multiplication | True | A zero factor gives zero; otherwise both factors are signs and their product is 1 or -1. |
| c | Subtraction | False | 1-(-1)=2 is outside the set. |

The multiplication table, with both operand orders (-1,0,1), is:

| x times y | -1 | 0 | 1 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| -1 | 1 | 0 | -1 |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | -1 | 0 | 1 |

The case proof and this exhaustive table cover all nine ordered pairs.

## 922: Closure of Integers (four tasks)

| Task | Operation | Verdict | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | Addition | Closed | For all m,n in Z, m+n is an integer. |
| b | Subtraction | Closed | -n is an integer, so m-n=m+(-n) is an integer. |
| c | Multiplication | Closed | The product of integer magnitudes is integral, and either product sign preserves integrality. |
| d | Division | Not closed | 1/2 is not an integer, with both operands integral and divisor nonzero. |

The source does not explicitly exclude zero in d. If the definition in 921
is applied literally to every pair, (1,0) separately obstructs a total
division operation: 1/0 is undefined, not a defined result outside Z.
If nonzero divisors are implicitly intended, 1/2 still refutes closure.
Neither issue authorizes replacing ordinary division with rounded integer
division or a quotient-and-remainder operation.

## 923: Three Sets Times Four Operations (12 tasks)

Let E={2m:m in Z}, O={2m+1:m in Z}, and Q be the rational numbers.
E includes zero and negative even integers; O includes negative odd integers
but not zero. The source explicitly permits only nonzero divisors.

| Task | Set | Operation | Verdict | Proof or counterexample |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a.add | E | Addition | Closed | 2m+2n=2(m+n). |
| a.subtract | E | Subtraction | Closed | 2m-2n=2(m-n). |
| a.multiply | E | Multiplication | Closed | (2m)(2n)=2(2mn). |
| a.divide | E | Division | Not closed | 2/4=1/2 is not even; alternatively 2/2=1 is not even. |
| b.add | O | Addition | Not closed | 1+1=2 is even; generally the sum is 2(m+n+1). |
| b.subtract | O | Subtraction | Not closed | 1-1=0 is even; generally the difference is 2(m-n). |
| b.multiply | O | Multiplication | Closed | (2m+1)(2n+1)=2(2mn+m+n)+1. |
| b.divide | O | Division | Not closed | 1/3 is not an odd integer; its divisor is a nonzero odd integer. |
| c.add | Q | Addition | Closed | (a/b)+(c/d)=(ad+bc)/(bd). |
| c.subtract | Q | Subtraction | Closed | (a/b)-(c/d)=(ad-bc)/(bd). |
| c.multiply | Q | Multiplication | Closed | (a/b)(c/d)=ac/(bd). |
| c.divide | Q | Division | Closed on nonzero divisors | (a/b)/(c/d)=ad/(bc), with c nonzero. |

The even and odd closure formulas hold for arbitrary integer m,n, so they
are universal proofs, not conclusions inferred from finite samples.
Every nonclosure counterexample uses two operands from the required set.
Both displayed division counterexamples have nonzero divisors.

For the rational proofs, a,b,c,d are integers and b,d are nonzero.
Thus ad+bc, ad-bc, ac, and ad are integers, and bd is nonzero.
For division the required nonzero rational divisor c/d implies c is nonzero,
so bc is also nonzero. Every result is therefore a ratio of integers with
a nonzero denominator. The proof includes zero numerators and negative
operands. Rejecting rational division due to a zero divisor would contradict
the explicit restriction in this exercise.

## Evidence Boundary

One bounded finite-set/exact-math execution is permitted after these solutions
are recorded. Its script and actual receipt are separate files in this folder.
The execution can exhaustively check finite 921, check concrete geometry and
counterexamples, and check exact rational identities on a bounded domain.
Finite samples are not a replacement for the universal proofs above.
Any execution failure must remain in the receipt; no successful check is rerun.
Actual receipt status, artifact hashes, usage, and unknown telemetry are reported
in FIRST.json after that execution. No later comparison is authorized here.
